// THE COLUMN DECLARATION SITE — THE REFUSED THING IS THE CLASS, NOT `Option`.
//
// ⚠ MEASURED ON THIS EXACT SOURCE BEFORE THE REFUSAL EXISTED: compiled rc 0,
// ran rc 0, and `group by s.m` over four rows carrying three distinct values
// answered TWO groups — one per VARIANT — while the `i64` control column `c` in
// the same struct answered three. Byte for byte the `Option<i64>` result in
// wql_column_decl_option_col_fail, from a hand-written enum that shares no name
// with it. So the defect is a property of PAYLOAD-CARRYING TAGGED VALUES, and a
// check that refused the spelling `Option` would be refusing a spelling.
//
// ⚠ AND THE COMPANION FACT, WHICH IS WHY THE CHECK ASKS THE VARIANTS RATHER
// THAN THE KIND: a FIELDLESS enum column is NOT this defect. Measured — select,
// select distinct, order by and group by all answer correctly over one,
// including with explicit non-contiguous discriminants — and it is admitted,
// pinned by pass/wql_column_decl_fieldless_enum_e2e. `enum` is not the
// antecedent; a variant with a payload is.
//
// The mechanism is the one in wql_column_decl_option_col_fail: the EL's key
// vector is `Vec<i64>`, an out-of-lattice column is spelled `i64` by
// `el::el_ty_of_name`, and the eight bytes read off a tagged box are its
// discriminant.
package test;

use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

enum M { S(i64), N }

struct Row { pub m: M, pub c: i64, pub id: i64 }

pub deem qm(rs: &[Row]) { from rs r group by r.m aggregate n = count() select n }

fn main() -> i32 { return 0i32; }
